
  <section xml:id="Math2000Exam2Practice">
    <title>Determined Ants</title>

  <subsection>
    <title>Matrix Computations</title>
    <p>The focus on this exercise will be on computing things about some specific small matrices.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-1-1">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp; 1 \\ 1  &amp; 0\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>B = \begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp;1 \\ -1 &amp;0 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
        <p><ol marker="(a)">
          <li><p>Compute <m>A + B</m></p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>B^{4}</m></p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>AB</m></p></li>
          <li><p>What is the determinant of <m>B</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Is <m>A^{10}</m> invertible?</p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>For (a), <me>A+B = \begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp; 1 \\ 1  &amp; 0\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp;1 \\ -1 &amp;0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp;2 \\ 0 &amp;0 \end{pmatrix}.</me> For (b): <me>B^{4} = (B^{2})^{2} = \left(\begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp;1 \\ -1 &amp;0 \end{pmatrix}^{2}\right)^{2} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 &amp;0 \\ 0 &amp;-1 \end{pmatrix}^{2} = I_{2}.</me> For (c), <me>AB = \begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp; 1 \\ 1  &amp; 0\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp;1 \\ -1 &amp;0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 &amp;0 \\ 0 &amp; 1\end{pmatrix}.</me> For (d), <me>\text{det}\begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp;1 \\ -1 &amp;0 \end{pmatrix} = 0^{2} - (-1)(1) = 1.</me> For (e), <me>\text{det}(A^{10}) = \text{det}(A)^{10} = \text{det}\begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp; 1 \\ 1  &amp; 0\end{pmatrix}^{10} = (-1)^{10} = 1.</me></p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-1-2">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} a &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; a \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>B = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp;2 \\ 3 &amp; 4 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
        <p><ol marker="(a)">
          <li><p>Compute <m>A^{10}</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>AB</m></p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>BA</m></p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>AB - BA</m></p></li>
          <li><p>For what values of <m>a</m> is <m>A</m> invertible? Explain.</p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>For (a), since products of diagonal matrices just correspond to multiplying the elements on the diagonal, <me>A^{10} = \begin{pmatrix} a &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; a \end{pmatrix}^{10} = \begin{pmatrix} a^{10} &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; a^{10} \end{pmatrix}.</me> For (b), <me>AB = \begin{pmatrix} a &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; a \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp;2 \\ 3 &amp; 4 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} a &amp;2a \\ 3a &amp; 4a \end{pmatrix}.</me> For (c), <me>BA =\begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp;2 \\ 3 &amp; 4 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} a &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; a \end{pmatrix}  = \begin{pmatrix} a &amp;2a \\ 3a &amp; 4a \end{pmatrix}.</me> For (d), <m>AB = BA</m>, so <m>AB-BA = \begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp;0\end{pmatrix}</m>. For (e), note that <me>\text{det}(A) = \text{det}\begin{pmatrix} a &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; a \end{pmatrix} = a^{2}.</me> Then <m>A</m> is invertible, if and only if <m>a^{2} = \text{det}(A) \neq 0</m>, which is true if and only if <m>a \neq 0</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-1-3">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
        <p><ol marker="(a)">
          <li><p>Compute <m>A + (A^{\intercal})^{\intercal}</m></p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>AA^{\intercal}</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>A^{\intercal}A</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Is <m>A A^{\intercal}</m> invertible? Explain.</p></li>
          <li><p>Is <m>A^{\intercal} A</m> invertible? Explain.</p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>For (a), <m>(A^{\intercal})^{\intercal} = A</m>, so <me>A +(A^{\intercal})^{\intercal} = A+A = 2A = \begin{pmatrix} 2 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\0 &amp; 2 &amp; 0 \end{pmatrix}.</me> For (b), <me>AA^{\intercal} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0  \\0 &amp; 1 \\0&amp;0\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0\\0 &amp; 1 \end{pmatrix}.</me> For (c), <me>A^{\intercal} A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0  \\0 &amp; 1 \\0&amp;0\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0\\ 0 &amp;0 &amp; 0 \end{pmatrix}</me> For (d), <m>A A^{\intercal} = I_{2}</m>, which is invertible. For (e), <m>A^{\intercal}A</m> has a row of zeroes and is therefore not invertible.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="section-2">
    <title>Compute Determinants</title>
    <p>In this exercise, I will give you three matrices defined implicitly somehow or explicitly, and you will need to either compute their determinants or explain why it's not possible to do so.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-2-1">
      <statement>
        <p>Compute the determinants of the following matrices or explain why you can't. Explain your answers:</p>
        <p><ol marker="(a)">
          <li><p><me>\begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 \\ 2 &amp; 2 &amp; 2 \\ 3 &amp; 3 &amp; 3 \end{pmatrix}</me></p></li>
          <li><p><me>\begin{pmatrix} 6 &amp; 0 &amp; 0\\ 0 &amp; 7 &amp; 0 \\0 &amp; 0 &amp;8 \end{pmatrix}</me></p></li>
          <li><p><m>A</m>, where <m>A</m> is obtained from the identity matrix by the sequence of operations: Replacement of first row minus the second, Scaling row <m>3</m> by <m>3</m>, Interchanging rows one and three, Scaling row <m>1</m> by <m>1/3</m>, and Replacing row <m>2</m> by row <m>2</m> minus row <m>3</m>.</p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>For (a), apply row replacement to subtract three times the first row from the third row. The resulting matrix has an all <m>0</m> row and so has determinant <m>0</m>. Replacement does not change the determinant, so the whole matrix has determinant <m>0</m>.</p>
        <p>For (b), the determinant of a diagonal matrix is the product of the diagonal entries, which is <m>6(7)(8)</m> in this case.</p>
        <p>For (c), the replacements don't change anything, so we can ignore them. There is one interchange, and a scaling by <m>3</m> and <m>1/3</m>, so the determinant in the end is <m>-1</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-2-2">
      <statement>
        <p>Compute the determinants of the following matrices or explain why you can't. Explain your answers:</p>
        <p><ol marker="(a)">
          <li><p><me>\begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 3 &amp; 4 \\ 0&amp; 0 &amp; 2 \\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp;1 \end{pmatrix}</me></p></li>
          <li><p><me>\begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 2 &amp;4 \\ 0 &amp; 2&amp; 6 \end{pmatrix}</me></p></li>
          <li><p><m>A^{4}</m>, where <me>A = \begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp;0 &amp; 1\\ 0 &amp;1 &amp; 0 \\ 1 &amp; 0 &amp;0\end{pmatrix}</me></p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>For (a), the matrix is upper triangular, so the determinant is the product of the diagonal entries, which is <m>0</m>.</p>
        <p>For (b), it is not square, so you can't take the determinant.</p>
        <p>For (c), <m>A</m> is one interchange away from <m>I_{3}</m> and so has determinant <m>-1</m>. Then <me>\text{det}(A^{4}) = \text{det}(A)^{4} = (-1)^{4} = 1.</me></p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-2-3">
      <statement>
        <p>Compute the determinants of the following matrices or explain why you can't. Explain your answers:</p>
        <p><ol marker="(a)">
          <li><p><me>\begin{pmatrix} 1&amp;1&amp;1 \\1&amp;2 &amp; 3 \\ 2&amp;3&amp; 4 \end{pmatrix}</me></p></li>
          <li><p><m>AB</m>, where <me>A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 2 &amp;4 \\ 0 &amp; 2&amp; 6 \\ 0&amp; 0 &amp;3 \end{pmatrix} \text{ and } B = \begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp; 1/3 &amp; 0 \\ 1/2 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp;0 &amp;1 \end{pmatrix}.</me></p></li>
          <li><p><m>C</m> where <m>C</m> is a <m>10 \times 10</m> matrix obtained from the identity matrix <m>I_{10}</m> by applying <m>13</m> interchanges.</p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>For (a), the third row is the sum of the first <m>2</m>, so the rows are linearly dependent. It follows that the matrix is not invertible, and so the matrix has determinant <m>0</m>.</p>
        <p>For (b), <m>A</m> is upper triangular, so its determinant is the product of its diagonal entries, which is <m>1(2)(3) = 6</m>. After interchanging, <m>B</m> is diagonal with entries <m>1/2, 1/3,1</m> and so has determinant <m>-1(1/2)(1/3)(1) = -1/6</m>. Hence, <m>\text{det}(AB) =\text{det}(A)\text{det}(B) = 6(-1/6) = -1.</m></p>
        <p>For (c), each interchange yields a multiplication by <m>-1</m>, so the determinant is <m>(-1)^{13} = -1</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="section-3">
    <title>Is the matrix invertible?</title>
    <p>In this exercise, I will give you 5 situations. In each, there are three possibilities:</p>
        <p><ol marker="(a)">
          <li><p>The matrix <m>A</m> is definitely invertible.</p></li>
          <li><p>The matrix <m>A</m> is definitely not invertible.</p></li>
          <li><p>There's not enough information to determine either way.</p></li>
        </ol></p>
    <p>Your job will be to determine which situation of the <m>3</m> you are in and justify why.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-3-1">
      <statement>
        <p>Which of the following conditions guarantee a matrix is invertible, guarantee it is not invertible, or does not give enough information to determine one way or another? Explain why for each.</p>
        <p><ol marker="(a)">
          <li><p><m>A</m> is <m>2 \times 4</m> with rank <m>2</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>A</m> is <m>3 \times 3</m> with determinant <m>10</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>A</m> is <m>n \times n</m> and there exist <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> both <m>n \times n</m> such that <m>BAC = I_{n}</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>A</m> is <m>n \times n</m> and <m>A^{\intercal}</m> is upper triangular</p></li>
          <li><p><m>A</m> is <m>n \times n</m> and <m>A = A^{\intercal}</m></p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>For (a), it is not invertible, since only square matrices are invertible. For (b), it is invertible, since its determinant is nonzero. For (c), since <m>B</m> is invertible, <m>AC = B^{-1} BA C = B^{-1} I_{n} = B^{-1}</m>. Then <m>AC B=  B^{-1} B = I_{n}</m>. Hence, <m>A</m> is invertible, with inverse <m>CB</m>. For (d), there is not enough information to determine it. For example, the zero matrix is such that <me>\begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 0\end{pmatrix}^{\intercal} =  \begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 0\end{pmatrix},</me> and the zero matrix is upper triangular, but it is not invertible. However, the same holds for <m>I_{2}</m>. It is upper triangular and invertible. Therefore, there is not enough to determine it. For (e), the same two examples work.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-3-2">
      <statement>
        <p>Which of the following conditions guarantee a matrix is invertible, guarantee it is not invertible, or does not give enough information to determine one way or another? Explain why for each.</p>
        <p><ol marker="(a)">
          <li><p><m>A</m> is <m>4 \times 4</m>, and the rows and columns of <m>A</m> all sum to <m>1</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>A</m> is <m>4 \times 4</m>, and the rows and columns of <m>A</m> all sum to <m>0</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>The columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>A</m> is <m>2 \times 2</m>, and <m>\text{det}(A - I_{2}) = 0</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>10 A</m> is invertible.</p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>For (a), this is not enough information to determine it. The identity matrix <m>I_{4}</m> satisfies this and is invertible. However, the <m>4 \times 4</m> matrix with all entries <m>1/4</m> satisfies that and is not invertible.</p>
        <p>For (b), it is never invertible. There are various ways to see this, but for example, one can apply three replacements adding row 1 to row 4, then adding row 2 to row 4, and finally row 3 to row 4. After that row 4 would be equal to the sum of the rows, which is <m>(0,0,0,0)</m>. Thus, after applying row operations one finds a matrix with a row of all zeroes. Hence, it is never invertible.</p>
        <p>For (c), this is not enough information, since you need to know if <m>A</m> is a square matrix or not.</p>
        <p>For (d), it is not enough information to determine it. For example, if <m>A = I_{2}</m>, then <m>A - I_{2}</m> is the <m>2 \times 2</m> zero matrix and so has determinant <m>0</m>. Thus, <m>A</m> can be invertible. At the same time if <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 0 \end{pmatrix}</m>, it is not invertible, and <me>A -I_{2} =\begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 0 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; -1 \end{pmatrix}</me> The determinant of <m>A -I_{2}</m> in that case is still <m>0</m>. Thus, it is not enough information to determine it.</p>
        <p>For (e), there are various ways to see this. For example, <m>A</m> is row equivalent to <m>10A</m> by rescaling each row by a factor of <m>10</m>. Hence, since <m>10A</m> is invertible, <m>A</m> must also be invertible.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-3-3">
      <statement>
        <p>Which of the following conditions guarantee a matrix is invertible, guarantee it is not invertible, or does not give enough information to determine one way or another? Explain why for each.</p>
        <p><ol marker="(a)">
          <li><p><m>A</m> is <m>n \times n</m>, and the reduced row echelon form of <m>A</m> has <m>n</m> pivot columns</p></li>
          <li><p><m>A</m> is <m>n \times n</m>, and <m>A \mathbf{x} = 0</m> has a unique solution</p></li>
          <li><p><m>A</m> is <m>n \times n</m>, and every entry on the diagonal of <m>A</m> is equal to <m>1</m> for <m>n \geq 2</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>A</m> is <m>3 \times 3</m>, and all <m>2 \times 2</m> subdeterminants of <m>A</m> are nonzero.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>A</m> is <m>3 \times 3</m> and <m>A = A^{2}</m></p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>For (a), the answer is yes. It implies that the reduced row echelon form has to be the identity matrix.</p>
        <p>For (b), the answer is also yes. It implies the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent meaning that the nullity is <m>0</m>, so the rank is <m>n</m>, which implies invertibility for an <m>n \times n</m> matrix.</p>
        <p>For (c), there is not enough information. The identity matrix <m>I_{n}</m> satisfies this and is invertible. However, the all <m>1</m>'s matrix satisfies this but is not invertible, since one row replacement yields a row of all <m>0</m>'s.</p>
        <p>For (d), there is not enough information to determine it. Note that the determinant of the matrix: <me>\begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1\\ 0&amp; 1 &amp; 1 \\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1\end{pmatrix}</me> is <m>1</m> and so it is invertible. Then applying two replacements adding the first row to the latter two rows to get the matrix <me>B = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1\\ 1&amp; 2 &amp; 2 \\ 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 2\end{pmatrix}</me> Define <m>A = B^{-1}</m>. Then the entries are, up to a nonzero constant scaling, exactly the <m>2 \times 2</m> subdeterminants of <m>A</m>. Hence, the <m>2 \times 2</m> subdeterminants of <m>A</m> are all nonzero in that case. For a noninvertible example, take <me>\begin{pmatrix} 
 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 \\0 &amp;1 &amp;1
 \\ 1 &amp;1 &amp; 2
\end{pmatrix}</me> The last row is the sum of the previous two rows meaning the rows are linearly dependent and so it is not invertible. By checking each <m>2 \times 2</m> subdeterminant, you can verify they are all nonzero.</p>
        <p>For (e), <m>I_{3} = I_{3}^{2}</m> and the zero matrix is equal to its square, so it is not enough information to determine that <m>A</m> is invertible.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="section-4">
    <title>Geometry</title>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-4-1">
      <statement>
        <p>Consider the linear transformation <m>T: \mathbb{R}^{2} \to \mathbb{R}^{2}</m> given by <me>T\left( \begin{matrix} x\\y \end{matrix}\right) = \begin{pmatrix} -\frac{1}{2}y \\ \frac{1}{2} x \end{pmatrix} .</me> Let <m>\vec{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
        <p><ol marker="(a)">
          <li><p>Find the matrix <m>A</m> of <m>T</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>A \vec{v}</m></p></li>
          <li><p>Describe what <m>T</m> does geometrically.</p></li>
          <li><p>Find the inverse <m>A^{-1}</m> of <m>A</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Describe what multiplication by <m>A^{-1}</m> does geometrically.</p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>For (a), the columns of the matrix are the images of the standard basis vectors. Since <me>T\left(\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\end{pmatrix}\right)=\begin{pmatrix}0\\\frac{1}{2}\end{pmatrix},\qquad T\left(\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\end{pmatrix}\right)=\begin{pmatrix}-\frac{1}{2}\\0\end{pmatrix},</me> we have <me>A=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-\frac{1}{2}\\\frac{1}{2}&amp;0\end{pmatrix}.</me></p>
        <p>For (b), <me>A\vec{v}=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-\frac{1}{2}\\\frac{1}{2}&amp;0\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}0\\\frac{1}{2}\end{pmatrix}.</me></p>
        <p>For (c), <m>T</m> rotates vectors counterclockwise by <m>90^\circ</m> and scales their lengths by a factor of <m>\frac12</m>.</p>
        <p>For (d), because the inverse must undo both operations, <me>A^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;2\\-2&amp;0\end{pmatrix}.</me> Indeed, <m>AA^{-1}=A^{-1}A=I_2</m>.</p>
        <p>For (e), multiplication by <m>A^{-1}</m> rotates vectors clockwise by <m>90^\circ</m> and scales their lengths by a factor of <m>2</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-4-2">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>\vec{u} = \begin{pmatrix} 1\\1  \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\vec{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 1\\0 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
        <p><ol marker="(a)">
          <li><p>Find a matrix <m>A</m> such that <m>A \vec{u} = 2\vec{u}</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Find a matrix <m>B</m> such that <m>B \vec{v} = 3\vec{v}</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Find a matrix <m>C</m> such that <m>C \vec{u} = 2 \vec{u}</m> and <m>C\vec{v} = 3 \vec{v}</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Explain why for such a matrix <m>C</m>, for any point <m>\vec{a}</m> on the <m>x</m>-axis, <m>C\vec{a}</m> is also on the <m>x</m>-axis.</p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>For (a), one choice is <m>A=2I_2=\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;0\\0&amp;2\end{pmatrix}</m>. Then <m>A\vec{u}=2\vec{u}</m>.</p>
        <p>For (b), one choice is <m>B=3I_2=\begin{pmatrix}3&amp;0\\0&amp;3\end{pmatrix}</m>. Then <m>B\vec{v}=3\vec{v}</m>.</p>
        <p>For (c), write <m>C=\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;b\\c&amp;d\end{pmatrix}</m>. Since <m>C\vec{v}=3\vec{v}</m>, the first column must be <m>\begin{pmatrix}3\\0\end{pmatrix}</m>. The condition <m>C\vec{u}=2\vec{u}</m> then gives <m>3+b=2</m> and <m>0+d=2</m>. Therefore, <me>C=\begin{pmatrix}3&amp;-1\\0&amp;2\end{pmatrix}.</me> Both conditions are satisfied.</p>
        <p>For (d), every point on the <m>x</m>-axis has the form <m>\vec{a}=t\vec{v}</m> for some real number <m>t</m>. By linearity, <me>C\vec{a}=C(t\vec{v})=tC\vec{v}=3t\vec{v}=\begin{pmatrix}3t\\0\end{pmatrix},</me> which is again on the <m>x</m>-axis.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-4-3">
      <statement>
        <p>Consider the pentagon with vertices: <m>(0,2), (2,1), (1,0), (-1,0), (-2,1)</m>.</p>
        <p><ol marker="(a)">
          <li><p>Draw the pentagon.</p></li>
          <li><p>Draw a subdivision of the pentagon into triangles.</p></li>
          <li><p>Find the area of the pentagon.</p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>For (a), plot the five given points and join them in the order <m>(0,2)</m>, <m>(2,1)</m>, <m>(1,0)</m>, <m>(-1,0)</m>, <m>(-2,1)</m>, returning to <m>(0,2)</m>.</p>
        <p>For (b), draw two diagonals from <m>(0,2)</m> to <m>(1,0)</m> and <m>(-1,0)</m>. This divides the pentagon into three triangles.</p>
        <p>For (c), use <m>(0,2)</m> as the reference point for each triangle. Each row is one of the other vertices minus <m>(0,2)</m>, with the coordinate differences shown explicitly:
          <me>\begin{aligned}
          A_{\text{right}} &amp;= \frac12\left|\det\begin{pmatrix}2-0 &amp; 1-2 \\ 1-0 &amp; 0-2\end{pmatrix}\right| = \frac12|{-3}| = \frac32,\\
          A_{\text{middle}} &amp;= \frac12\left|\det\begin{pmatrix}1-0 &amp; 0-2 \\ -1-0 &amp; 0-2\end{pmatrix}\right| = \frac12|{-4}| = 2,\\
          A_{\text{left}} &amp;= \frac12\left|\det\begin{pmatrix}-1-0 &amp; 0-2 \\ -2-0 &amp; 1-2\end{pmatrix}\right| = \frac12|{-3}| = \frac32.
          \end{aligned}</me>
          Therefore, <me>\text{Area}=A_{\text{right}}+A_{\text{middle}}+A_{\text{left}}=\frac32+2+\frac32=5.</me></p>
        <image width="65%">
          <shortdescription>Pentagon with five labeled vertices.</shortdescription>
          <latex-image>\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]
\draw[thick] (0,2) -- (2,1) -- (1,0) -- (-1,0) -- (-2,1) -- cycle;
\fill (0,2) circle (1.5pt) node[above] {$(0,2)$};
\fill (2,1) circle (1.5pt) node[right] {$(2,1)$};
\fill (1,0) circle (1.5pt) node[below right] {$(1,0)$};
\fill (-1,0) circle (1.5pt) node[below left] {$(-1,0)$};
\fill (-2,1) circle (1.5pt) node[left] {$(-2,1)$};
\end{tikzpicture}</latex-image>
        </image>
        <image width="65%">
          <shortdescription>Pentagon subdivided into three triangles by two diagonals from the top vertex.</shortdescription>
          <latex-image>\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]
\draw[thick] (0,2) -- (2,1) -- (1,0) -- (-1,0) -- (-2,1) -- cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,2) -- (1,0);
\draw[dashed] (0,2) -- (-1,0);
\fill (0,2) circle (1.5pt) node[above] {$(0,2)$};
\fill (2,1) circle (1.5pt) node[right] {$(2,1)$};
\fill (1,0) circle (1.5pt) node[below right] {$(1,0)$};
\fill (-1,0) circle (1.5pt) node[below left] {$(-1,0)$};
\fill (-2,1) circle (1.5pt) node[left] {$(-2,1)$};
\end{tikzpicture}</latex-image>
        </image>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
